ravel
=====

A toy JavaScript/TypeScript runtime written in Rust, built on QuickJS and
Oxc. It exists to learn how Node, Deno, and Bun work.

MIT licensed. See LICENSE.


Usage
-----

	ravel file.js           run JavaScript
	ravel file.ts           run TypeScript
	ravel                   start the REPL
	ravel --build file.js   build mode (one-off, no timers)
	ravel --serve           serve dist/ on port 3000
	ravel --serve 8080      serve dist/ on another port
	ravel --help
	ravel --version


Building
--------

	cargo build             build to target/debug/ravel
	cargo install --path .  install `ravel` on your PATH
	cargo test              run the tests

`cargo build` only writes to target/debug/. Run `cargo install --path .`
to update the `ravel` on your PATH.


Features
--------

ES6+ via QuickJS
	Promises, async/await, classes, modules.

TypeScript
	Oxc strips types from .ts and .tsx files.

JSX
	.tsx files render JSX to HTML strings. See "JSX" below.

Async runtime
	setTimeout, setInterval, clearTimeout, clearInterval on Tokio.
	Promise callbacks and await continuations run before exit.

Error reporting
	Uncaught errors and unhandled rejections print to stderr and exit
	1. See "Error reporting" below.

Sandboxed fs
	fs.readFile, fs.writeFile, fs.mkdirSync, fs.exists. All are scoped
	to the script's directory. Path traversal, symlink escape, and
	null bytes are blocked.

Text encoding
	TextEncoder and TextDecoder. UTF-8 only; other labels throw.

console
	log, info, debug to stdout. warn, error to stderr.

ESM imports
	Relative and bare imports resolve .js, .mjs, .ts, .tsx.

Globals
	__filename, __dirname, process.env, process.argv, process.exit,
	ravel.version, ravel.build.

REPL
	Line history, saved between sessions. Timers work.

Build mode
	--build runs a script once, with ravel.build === true.

Dev server
	--serve serves dist/ over HTTP. --base strips a path prefix.


Project structure
-----------------

	src/
	  core.rs       module declarations for core/
	  core/         QuickJS runtime, module loader, event loop
	  transpiler.rs Oxc TypeScript and JSX transpiler
	  jsx.rs        note() HTML renderer
	  console.rs    console log/info/debug/warn/error
	  encoding.rs   TextEncoder / TextDecoder
	  error.rs      error formatting, unhandled rejections
	  fs.rs         sandboxed filesystem
	  timer.rs      setTimeout / setInterval
	  config.rs     ravel.json config loader
	  cli.rs        module declarations for cli/
	  cli/          CLI entry point
	examples/       usage demos
	tests/          integration + snapshot tests


Error reporting
---------------

Uncaught errors print the name, message, and user stack frames to stderr.
The process exits 1, so CI sees the failure.

	$ ravel err.js
	Uncaught TypeError: cannot read property 'x' of null
	    at inner (err.js:1:18)
	    at outer (err.js:2:19)
	    at <anonymous> (err.js:3:1)
	$ echo $?
	1

Unhandled promise rejections print the same way:

	$ ravel rej.js
	Unhandled promise rejection: Error: nope
	    at <anonymous> (rej.js:1:19)

A rejection with a handler is not reported. This includes handlers
attached later, as await does. See examples/errors.js.


JSX
---

.tsx files are transpiled and run automatically:

	// example.tsx
	const el = <div class="card"><h1>Hello</h1></div>;
	console.log(el);
	// <div class="card"><h1>Hello</h1></div>

	function Badge(props) {
	  return <span class="badge">{props.text}</span>;
	}
	console.log(<Badge text="New" />);
	// <span class="badge">New</span>

Oxc transforms JSX into note() calls. note() renders to HTML strings. It
supports attributes, nested children, void tags (<br>, <img>), fragments
(<>...</>), function components, and attribute escaping.


Build mode
----------

--build runs a script once as a build task. It sets ravel.build to true,
sets process.env.RAVEL_BUILD to "1", and skips the timer loop.

	// build.js
	if (ravel.build) {
	  fs.writeFile("dist/index.html", "<h1>Built</h1>");
	}

fs.writeFile takes a string. Bytes work too, for binary output:

	fs.writeFile("dist/index.html",
	             new TextEncoder().encode("<h1>Built</h1>"));

Build scripts can use ESM imports, JSX, and fs.mkdirSync:

	// components.tsx
	export function Layout(props) {
	  return (
	    <html lang="en">
	      <head><title>{props.title}</title></head>
	      <body>{props.children}</body>
	    </html>
	  );
	}

	// build.tsx
	import { Layout } from "./components.tsx";

	fs.mkdirSync("dist/blog");
	fs.writeFile(
	  "dist/index.html",
	  "<!DOCTYPE html>" + <Layout title="Home"><h1>Welcome</h1></Layout>,
	);

Then:

	ravel --build build.tsx

See examples/site/ for a full multi-page example.


Dev server
----------

--serve serves the output of --build:

	ravel --serve               dist/ on port 3000
	ravel --serve 8080          dist/ on port 8080
	ravel --base /repo --serve  strip the /repo prefix

It serves dist/ from the current directory. Unknown paths fall back to
dist/index.html.

Use --base when the site lives under a subpath, such as GitHub Pages at
username.github.io/repo-name/. It strips the prefix, so /repo-name/style.css
resolves to dist/style.css.


Configuration
-------------

ravel.json in the project root sets defaults. CLI flags win.

	{
	  "base": "/my-repo",
	  "port": 8080
	}

	Field  Type    Default  Description
	base   string  ""       Path prefix to strip when serving
	port   number  3000     Dev server port


Filesystem
----------

fs.writeFile creates parent directories:

	// creates dist/posts/hello/ too
	fs.writeFile("dist/posts/hello/index.html", "<h1>Hello</h1>");

fs.mkdirSync creates directories recursively, inside the sandbox:

	fs.mkdirSync("dist/assets/css");
	fs.mkdirSync("dist");             // no-op if it exists


Globals
-------

Available in all modes:

	ravel.version             version string, e.g. "0.3.0"
	ravel.build               true under --build
	process.env               environment variables
	process.env.RAVEL_BUILD   "1" in build mode
	process.argv              [execPath, scriptPath, ...userArgs]
	process.exit(code)        exit with a status code


Roadmap
-------

The goal is learning, so this follows mechanisms, not features.

Async I/O
	Make fs return promises backed by Tokio, instead of blocking the
	thread. A Rust future resolves a JavaScript promise. Every runtime
	is built on this.

A real event loop
	Replace the 10ms polling loop. Sleep until the next timer deadline
	or I/O completion.

Module resolution
	node_modules lookup, package.json exports and imports maps,
	conditional exports, self-referencing imports.

fetch and web standards
	fetch, Request/Response, URL/URLSearchParams, crypto,
	AbortController, streams.

Multiple isolates
	Move the global timer state into the engine, so one process can
	hold several runtimes. Needed for Workers.
